## Overview

Buckso is a concept for a mobile personal finance assistant aimed at Gen Z. I designed it in response to a design challenge brief, working alone over six days. It brings together budgeting, savings goals, recurring payments, fact-checked learning content and an AI chat assistant, with a light layer of rewards to keep people coming back.

{% stats %}
{% stat value="6 days" label="Brief to hi-fi screens" /%}
{% stat value="2" label="Personas" /%}
{% stat value="5" label="Core app sections" /%}
{% /stats %}

## The problem

The brief said that financial literacy matters, but traditional finance tools can feel overwhelming for Gen Z. It asked for an AI-powered personal finance assistant app that helps Gen Z manage their money, and to consider how AI could personalise the experience, offer actionable insights and gamify saving and budgeting.

I broke the brief into three parts: the deliverable (an AI-powered finance assistant app), the target users (Gen Z) and the user goal (managing their finances well). That gave me a frame to check later decisions against.

## My role

This was a solo project. I did the desk research, synthesis, information architecture, wireframes, the visual identity and the high-fidelity screens in Figma.

Two constraints shaped the work. The first was time: six days from brief to screens. The second was that I had no access to primary research, so I couldn't interview or test with real users. I leaned on secondary sources instead and marked clearly where I was working from assumptions.

![Design process from understand to design](/work/genz-finance-assistant/design-process.webp "The four-stage process I followed: understand, define, ideate, design")

## Process

### Research

I started with desk research, with three goals: to understand what personal finance covers, to learn how Gen Z behaves around money, and to find where AI can genuinely help as a finance assistant.

Personal finance turned out to be broad, spanning income, budgeting, saving, debt, investing, insurance, retirement and tax. For Gen Z's perspective, I read through Quora, Reddit and blogs. A few patterns kept coming up. Easy EMI and buy now, pay later schemes have added to debt and stress. Many people take on side gigs to diversify their income. Retirement planning gets pushed aside for short-term goals. A lot of their financial information comes from uncertified influencers.

![Nine research insight cards about Gen Z and money](/work/genz-finance-assistant/research-insights.webp "Key findings from secondary research, including figures reported by Experian and Consumer Money Matter")

I also looked at what AI can realistically offer here: automatic categorisation of expenses, personalised insights based on spending habits, forecasting and goal setting, and conversational guidance through a chatbot.

### Insights

I pulled the research into an empathy map covering what Gen Z sees, hears, thinks and does around money. Some of the points on it came from my own assumptions, which I noted on the map itself.

![Empathy map with see, hear, think and feel, and say and do quadrants](/work/genz-finance-assistant/empathy-map.webp "Empathy map, partly based on assumptions")

From there I built two personas. Riya is a 23-year-old junior software engineer in Bangalore who finds it hard to stick to a budget because of social spending and online purchases, and who is paying off a student loan while juggling other goals. Arjun is a 27-year-old freelance graphic designer in Mumbai whose income changes month to month, and who struggles to keep business and personal expenses apart for tax purposes.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Persona: Riya Sharma, junior software engineer](/work/genz-finance-assistant/persona-riya.webp)

![Persona: Arjun Verma, freelance graphic designer](/work/genz-finance-assistant/persona-arjun.webp)
{% /gallery %}

Writing these two side by side made one thing clear. A fixed budget template wouldn't work for both of them. The app needed to adapt to the person's income pattern and goals, and explain things in plain language.

With that, I rewrote the brief in my own words:

{% callout %}
Design an AI-driven finance app that makes budgeting, saving and learning about money engaging and social for Gen Z, with a personalised and gamified experience that builds financial confidence while keeping their money safe.
{% /callout %}

### Ideation

I used a problem–solution matrix to turn the research into features. Trouble tracking spending led to AI expense categorisation and budget suggestions. A preference for short-term goals led to goal setting with visual progress. Low motivation led to savings challenges and rewards. Gaps in financial literacy and reliance on influencers led to fact-checked content and a way to reach a professional advisor. High debt led to debt tracking with repayment reminders.

These features became the information architecture. After sign-up and onboarding, the app splits into five areas: Home, Budget and Goals, Learning, Profile, and a central Add Transaction action, with Rewards and Notifications reachable from the header.

![Information architecture of the app](/work/genz-finance-assistant/information-architecture.webp "Information architecture built from the problem–solution matrix")

I then sketched low-fidelity wireframes for the main screens and tried a few different flows before settling the layout and bottom navigation.

![Grayscale wireframes of eight screens](/work/genz-finance-assistant/wireframes.webp "Wireframes used to explore flows before visual design")

## Solution

### Visual identity

I named the app Buckso and gave it the tagline "Get that money glow-up". The identity is dark, with a single electric lime accent (#CFF008) set against near-black greys. Urbanist is the heading font and DM Sans is the body font. I wanted it to feel closer to the apps Gen Z already uses every day than to a bank.

![Buckso logo, app icons, colour palette and typefaces](/work/genz-finance-assistant/visual-identity.webp "Visual identity: logo, palette and type")

### Key screens

**Home** opens with a personalised AI insight, such as how much the user saved this month and what that means for a goal. Below that are the total balance, monthly expenses, savings and a financial health score, followed by recent transactions, shortcuts to key features and a cash flow chart that can be filtered by account and date. The header shows notifications and in-app coins.

**Budget and Goals** sets the monthly budget from the previous month's spending, with an option to edit it, and shows progress by category. Users can create short- and long-term saving goals, and some goals earn coins when completed. Recurring payments such as EMIs, subscriptions and money owed to friends sit in the same place, with reminders.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Home screen with balance, health score, transactions and cash flow](/work/genz-finance-assistant/screen-home.webp)

![Budget and Goals screen with category budgets, saving goals and recurring payments](/work/genz-finance-assistant/screen-budget-goals.webp)
{% /gallery %}

**Add a transaction** is the centre button in the navigation. Users enter an amount, then pick the type, category and date, which defaults to today. The confirmation screen shows how much of that category's budget has been used and rewards consistent tracking with coins.

![Add transaction flow across three screens](/work/genz-finance-assistant/add-transaction.webp "Logging an expense, then seeing it against the category budget")

**Rewards** shows the user's coin balance, ways to redeem coins for rewards and offers, AI-personalised challenges like "save 20%", and a leaderboard with friends. **Learn and chat** carries fact-checked financial content, a way to connect with a professional advisor, and Buckso, the AI assistant users can chat with about tax, insurance, budgeting and other questions.

{% gallery columns=2 %}
![Rewards screen with coins, offers and challenges](/work/genz-finance-assistant/screen-rewards.webp)

![Learn and chat screen with fact-checked content and the Buckso assistant](/work/genz-finance-assistant/screen-learn-chat.webp)
{% /gallery %}

## Outcome & learnings

By the end of the six days I had a researched concept, an information architecture, wireframes, a visual identity and high-fidelity designs for the core screens. Not every screen was finished in that time. Onboarding, the full chat experience and the profile and tax sections are the obvious gaps.

The biggest limitation is that everything rests on secondary research and my own assumptions. Nothing was tested with users. With more time, I'd interview a few people like Riya and Arjun and run quick usability tests on the add-transaction flow and the home screen. I'd especially want to know whether people find the AI insights useful or intrusive, and whether the coins and leaderboard motivate them or just add noise.

Working to such a short deadline taught me to be strict about scope. The problem–solution matrix made that easier, because it linked every feature back to something I had found in the research, and that told me what to design first.
